These identities are the allied-angle results of Unit 10. A quick way to rebuild any of them: for \(\pi \pm \theta\) and \(2\pi - \theta\) the function name stays the same, and only the sign may change. The sign is the sign of the function in the quadrant where the angle lies (take \(\theta\) acute):
\(\pi - \theta\) lies in Quadrant II, where only sine is positive;
\(\pi + \theta\) lies in Quadrant III, where only tangent is positive;
\(2\pi - \theta\) and \(-\theta\) lie in Quadrant IV, where only cosine is positive.
